# Task context

## Notion (opt-in only)

Use Notion MCP **only** when the user:

- Pastes a `notion.so` link or Notion page URL, or
- Explicitly names a Notion page/database to open or update, or
- Clearly asks to read from or write to Notion.

**Otherwise:** do not search Notion, do not open the Work Journal, do not create Notion pages, and do not assume a task lives in Notion. Work from the user's message, repo files, and anything else they attach or @-mention.

When the user **does** provide a Notion link, treat that page as the task spec for that chat unless they say otherwise.

## Work areas (scope tags)

Use these area names when scoping work — they map to feature branches, not ticket prefixes:

| Area | Scope |
|---|---|
| `foundation` | Core scaffolding, services, layout |
| `solution-finder` | Solution finder modal (selection, targets, includes/excludes) |
| `left-sidebar` | Map layers panel, selected layers, layer controls |
| `map-spatial` | ArcGIS map panel |
| `analysis-dashboards` | Right sidebar, metrics, comparison |
| `aoi-dashboard` | Area of Interest panel (right sidebar); often with `analysis-dashboards` |
| `backend` | Server/API/data-pipeline — only when work is **not** the default Angular app stack |

**Default stack:** Assume client/UI (Angular + map + sidebars) unless `backend` applies.

## Verbal-review description template

When capturing bugs, polish, or review notes (in chat or in a doc the user asked for), use this structure:

1. **## Problem** — What is wrong or missing; why it matters (user impact).
2. **## Location** — Where in the app and/or repo (panel, file, element id).
3. **## Fix** — Intended direction; acceptance-style bullets if helpful.
4. **## Design / open questions** (optional) — Product/design sign-off or tradeoffs before implementation.
5. **## Related** — Links to related repo docs or pages the user provided.

## When working on a task

1. Use what the user gave you: message, Notion link (if any), file paths, screenshots.
2. Note the work area / tags to understand scope and branch.
3. Do not invent or fetch external task trackers unless the user points you at one.
